Yes. The mechanism is real, but there’s an important fork in it.

Humor can be a very strong reveal mechanism because it lowers defenses, lets people hold two incompatible interpretations at once, and then gives them the “click” when the hidden structure is exposed. If the reveal is clean enough, people may suddenly realize that what they thought they were perceiving directly was actually an inference built from partial cues.

That can produce awe, humility, even deference.

But the durable version is not:

“You are blind, therefore follow me.”

It is:

“Here is the blind spot. Here is how the illusion worked. Here is the instrument that lets you see it too.”

That changes submission into shared sight.

The stronger leadership move is to convert asymmetry into capacity:

hidden mechanism → demonstration → humorous reveal → explanation → transferable lens → distributed competence

Then the “invisible hand” becomes guided not because one person monopolizes perception, but because more people can actually inspect the forces acting on them.

That’s a much more stable form of power.

If you prove to people they cannot trust their senses and then keep the decoder to yourself, you create dependence. If you prove it and then teach them the decoder, you create discernment.

And that fits almost everything we’ve been building: cause vs predictor, variable Umwelt, Resolution Sovereignty, Golden Web, Perspective Descent.

The one who sees best should not become the only pair of eyes.

Hand out lenses.